WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.808
Here are a café's days.

2
00:00:04.891 --> 00:00:07.131
Temperature runs across and

3
00:00:07.214 --> 00:00:09.288
iced coffee sales run up

4
00:00:09.288 --> 00:00:12.192
both measured from their averages.

5
00:00:12.275 --> 00:00:14.515
Day A is two degrees warmer

6
00:00:14.598 --> 00:00:16.257
and ten cups busier.

7
00:00:16.257 --> 00:00:18.663
Day B is two degrees colder

8
00:00:18.663 --> 00:00:20.820
and also ten cups busier.

9
00:00:21.170 --> 00:00:23.868
Both days are the same ordinary

10
00:00:23.955 --> 00:00:27.174
distance from the average: root five.

11
00:00:27.174 --> 00:00:29.524
Which day is more unusual?

12
00:00:29.524 --> 00:00:31.090
Pause and decide.

13
00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:36.975
Start with one measurement.

14
00:00:36.975 --> 00:00:40.732
The bell curve has exponent minus one half

15
00:00:40.732 --> 00:00:42.952
times x minus mu squared

16
00:00:42.952 --> 00:00:44.660
over sigma squared

17
00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:47.905
a squared distance in units of sigma.

18
00:00:47.905 --> 00:00:50.466
A wider bell, with sigma two

19
00:00:50.466 --> 00:00:52.430
falls off more slowly.

20
00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:55.206
For a vector of measurements

21
00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:57.382
sigma squared becomes the

22
00:00:57.465 --> 00:00:59.473
covariance matrix Sigma

23
00:00:59.557 --> 00:01:02.234
and dividing becomes multiplying

24
00:01:02.318 --> 00:01:03.740
by Sigma inverse.

25
00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:07.422
For the café, Sigma is five, two, two, two

26
00:01:07.422 --> 00:01:10.517
and its inverse is one sixth of two

27
00:01:10.517 --> 00:01:12.860
minus two, minus two, five.

28
00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:15.517
The factor in front makes the

29
00:01:15.596 --> 00:01:17.346
total probability one.

30
00:01:17.346 --> 00:01:20.209
There, the square root of det Sigma

31
00:01:20.289 --> 00:01:22.197
plays the role of sigma

32
00:01:22.277 --> 00:01:24.663
a wider cloud spreads the same

33
00:01:24.743 --> 00:01:26.890
probability over more area.

34
00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:29.087
Back to the two days.

35
00:01:29.087 --> 00:01:30.704
The tempting answer

36
00:01:30.781 --> 00:01:32.784
the same ordinary distance

37
00:01:32.861 --> 00:01:34.555
means equally unusual.

38
00:01:34.555 --> 00:01:35.710
Is that right?

39
00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:39.494
Measure with Sigma inverse instead.

40
00:01:39.494 --> 00:01:41.965
For day A, the offset two

41
00:01:41.965 --> 00:01:45.901
one gives one sixth of eight, minus eight

42
00:01:45.901 --> 00:01:48.098
plus five: five sixths.

43
00:01:48.098 --> 00:01:50.111
For day B, minus two

44
00:01:50.111 --> 00:01:53.956
one gives one sixth of eight, plus eight

45
00:01:53.956 --> 00:01:56.610
plus five: three and a half.

46
00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:00.139
So day B is about four times as far

47
00:02:00.139 --> 00:02:03.583
in the distance that matches the data.

48
00:02:03.583 --> 00:02:05.437
It is cold and busy

49
00:02:05.437 --> 00:02:08.263
against the trend of the cloud

50
00:02:08.263 --> 00:02:11.000
while day A follows the trend.

51
00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:14.075
Two more days make the same point.

52
00:02:14.075 --> 00:02:17.201
Three degrees warmer, at normal sales

53
00:02:17.201 --> 00:02:19.605
has d squared equal to three.

54
00:02:19.605 --> 00:02:23.132
Twenty extra cups, at a normal temperature

55
00:02:23.132 --> 00:02:25.456
has three point three three

56
00:02:25.456 --> 00:02:28.822
although that day is only two units away.

57
00:02:28.822 --> 00:02:30.826
Temperature varies more

58
00:02:30.826 --> 00:02:33.150
so its units count for less.

59
00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:36.815
The points at one fixed distance

60
00:02:36.912 --> 00:02:38.455
form an ellipse.

61
00:02:38.455 --> 00:02:42.794
Its axes are the eigenvectors of Sigma, two

62
00:02:42.794 --> 00:02:45.108
one and one, minus two

63
00:02:45.204 --> 00:02:48.290
and its half axes are the square

64
00:02:48.386 --> 00:02:52.050
roots of the eigenvalues, six and one.

65
00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:54.867
Day A lies on the long axis

66
00:02:54.867 --> 00:02:57.158
inside the first ellipse.

67
00:02:57.158 --> 00:03:01.034
Day B lies outside it, close to the second.

68
00:03:01.034 --> 00:03:02.708
In two dimensions

69
00:03:02.708 --> 00:03:05.352
the ellipses of distance one

70
00:03:05.352 --> 00:03:08.171
two and three hold thirty nine

71
00:03:08.259 --> 00:03:10.550
eighty six and ninety nine

72
00:03:10.638 --> 00:03:12.400
percent of the days.

73
00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:16.215
Every Gaussian is a stretched white one.

74
00:03:16.215 --> 00:03:19.207
Factor Sigma as L times L transpose

75
00:03:19.207 --> 00:03:22.684
the Cholesky factor of Lecture twenty two.

76
00:03:22.684 --> 00:03:26.242
The squares of L's diagonal are the pivots

77
00:03:26.242 --> 00:03:27.940
five and six fifths.

78
00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:31.580
Start with white noise z: a round cloud

79
00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:34.047
whose level sets are circles.

80
00:03:34.047 --> 00:03:35.281
Multiply by L

81
00:03:35.281 --> 00:03:38.818
and the circle becomes the café's ellipse.

82
00:03:38.818 --> 00:03:42.108
Going back, L inverse whitens the data

83
00:03:42.108 --> 00:03:45.562
and the distance becomes ordinary length

84
00:03:45.644 --> 00:03:47.947
day A whitens to a vector of

85
00:03:48.029 --> 00:03:50.250
squared length five sixths.

86
00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:53.008
This is also how computers

87
00:03:53.101 --> 00:03:57.177
draw Gaussian samples: take white numbers z

88
00:03:57.177 --> 00:03:59.400
and return mu plus L z.

89
00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:03.318
Suppose tomorrow will be twenty degrees

90
00:04:03.318 --> 00:04:05.070
three above average.

91
00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:08.158
What should the café expect to sell?

92
00:04:08.241 --> 00:04:10.411
The best guess follows the

93
00:04:10.495 --> 00:04:12.581
line of slope two fifths

94
00:04:12.664 --> 00:04:14.834
so sales are five plus two

95
00:04:14.917 --> 00:04:17.755
fifths times three: six point two

96
00:04:17.755 --> 00:04:19.340
or sixty two cups.

97
00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:21.793
The spread that remains is

98
00:04:21.874 --> 00:04:24.624
two minus four fifths: six fifths.

99
00:04:24.705 --> 00:04:26.970
Two fifths is the multiplier

100
00:04:27.051 --> 00:04:28.992
of elimination on Sigma

101
00:04:28.992 --> 00:04:31.904
and six fifths is its second pivot.

102
00:04:31.904 --> 00:04:34.250
Conditioning is elimination.

103
00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:36.220
A simulation agrees

104
00:04:36.301 --> 00:04:39.459
simulated days near twenty degrees sell

105
00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:42.050
six point one eight on average

106
00:04:42.050 --> 00:04:44.560
with spread one point two one.

107
00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:48.477
The Gaussian's exponent is minus one half

108
00:04:48.558 --> 00:04:51.732
of a quadratic form with Sigma inverse

109
00:04:51.732 --> 00:04:54.499
the squared Mahalanobis distance.

110
00:04:54.581 --> 00:04:56.778
Its level sets are ellipses

111
00:04:56.859 --> 00:04:58.731
along the eigenvectors.

112
00:04:58.813 --> 00:05:01.661
A Cholesky factor turns white noise

113
00:05:01.742 --> 00:05:03.696
into the cloud and back

114
00:05:03.777 --> 00:05:06.381
and conditioning on one variable

115
00:05:06.463 --> 00:05:08.660
is one step of elimination.

116
00:05:09.010 --> 00:05:11.864
Next: the same Sigma inverse

117
00:05:11.864 --> 00:05:15.210
used as weights in least squares.
